{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-11007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+11$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-22x-59=(Ax+B)(x-5)+C({x}^{2}+11).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2}-22(5)-59 & =(A(5)+B)(5-5)+C({5}^{2}+11) \\\\ 25-110-59 & =36C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{144}{36} \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-22(0)-59 & =(A(0)+B)(0-5)+C({0}^{2}+11) \\\\ -59 & =-5B+11C \\\\ -59 & =-5B+11(-4) \\\\ 5B & =-44+59 \\\\ B & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-22(1)-59 & =(A(1)+B)(1-5)+C({1}^{2}+11) \\\\ 1-22-59 & =-4A-4B+12C \\\\ -80 & =-4A-4(3)+12(-4) \\\\ 4A & =-12-48+80 \\\\ A & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=5,$ $B=3,$ and $C=-4.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)}=\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73d1f7967d0cc3d206837287dcbcbaa2042b00b226d67b913258704e804e79b0"} {"question" "q-11007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{7}{(x-5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{3x+2}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{1}{(x-5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{3}{(x-5)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\frac{{x}^{2}-43x-20}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-11)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-43x-20}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-11)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-11)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+3)(x-11)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-43x-20=(Ax+B)(x-11)+C({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=11$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(11)^{2}-43(11)-20 & =(A(11)+B)(11-11)+C((11)^{2}+3) \\\\ 121-473-20 & =124C \\\\ -372 & =124C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{372}{124} \\\\ C & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-43(0)-20 & =(A(0)+B)(0-11)+C({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ -20 & =-11B+3C \\\\ -20 & =-11B+3(-3) \\\\ 11B & =-9+20 \\\\ B & =\\frac{11}{11} \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-43(1)-20 & =(A(1)+B)(1-11)+C({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ -62 & =-10A-10B+4C \\\\ -62 & =-10A-10(1)+4(-3) \\\\ 10A & =-10-12+62 \\\\ A & =\\frac{40}{10} \\\\ A & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=4,$ $B=1,$ and $C=-3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-43x-20}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-11)}=\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{3}{(x-11)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73d1f7967d0cc3d206837287dcbcbaa2042b00b226d67b913258704e804e79b0"} {"question" "q-11013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2x+9}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{6}{(x-11)}"] ["math" "\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{3}{(x-11)}"] ["math" "\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-11)}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+4}{({x}^{2}+3)}+\\frac{7}{(x-11)}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+5}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{4}{(x-11)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{3}{(x-11)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+2x+5=(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+2x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+2(1)+5 & =(A(1)+B)(1-1)+C((1)^{2}+2(1)+3) \\\\ 5+2+5 & =6C \\\\ C & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({0}^{2})+2(0)+5 & =(A(0)+B)(0-1)+C({0}^{2}+2(0)+3) \\\\ 5 & =-B+3C \\\\ 5 & =-B+3(2) \\\\ B & =6-5 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2)^{2}+2(2)+5 & =(A(2)+B)(2-1)+C({2}^{2}+2(2)+3) \\\\ 29 & =2A+B+11C \\\\ 29 & =2A+(1)+11(2) \\\\ 2A & =29-23 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=1,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)}=\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73d1f7967d0cc3d206837287dcbcbaa2042b00b226d67b913258704e804e79b0"} {"question" "q-11014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+3}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+3}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}-\\frac{2}{(x-1)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-11014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-49724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+9}{({x}^{2}+3)x}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, find the value of $A+B+C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-49724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We multiply both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+3)x$ and get\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+9=(Ax+B)x+C({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+9 & =(A(0)+B)(0)+C({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ 9 & =3C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us an equation for $A$ in terms of $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+9 & =(A(1)+B)(1)+(3)({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ 11 & =A+B+12 \\\\ A & =-B-1.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$ and using our result for $C$ gives us another equation for $A$ in terms of $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)^{2}+9 & =(A(-1)+B)(-1)+(3)((-1)^{2}+3) \\\\ 11 & =A-B+12 \\\\ A & =B-1.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the equations $(1)$ and $(2)$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-B-1 & =B-1 \\\\ -2B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we use our result for $B$ in equation $(2).$ We get\n\n$$\nA=(0)-1=-1.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1,$ $B=0,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\nA+B+C=-1+0+3=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-49724" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73d1f7967d0cc3d206837287dcbcbaa2042b00b226d67b913258704e804e79b0"} {"question" "q-49724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-49724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "11"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-49724" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-102560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5x+12}{(2{x}^{2}+3)(x-3)}=\\frac{x+B}{2{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{2}{x-3},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-102560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(2{x}^{2}+3)(x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+12 & =(x+B)(x-3)+2(2{x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)^{2}-5(0)+12 & =(0+B)(0-3)+2(2(0)^{2}+3) \\\\ 12 & =-3B+6 \\\\ 6 & =-3B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-102560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73d1f7967d0cc3d206837287dcbcbaa2042b00b226d67b913258704e804e79b0"} {"question" "q-102560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978716/state.json" "file_sha256" "040684f799b67c9f534fd6fd4f91a37371bf85c0a422b71ac1c53670d18b5ca7"} {"question" "q-102560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] [